  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale  \nThe current approach suffers from being overly reliant on sequential refinement from parent solutions, which can lead to plateauing in local optima. The proposed method adopts a hybrid multi-resolution strategy combined with adaptive interpolation between hierarchical representations. Key innovations:  \n   - Generative adversarial refinement: contrast between synthetic and original samples to escape local minima  \n   - Hierarchical optimization: enable learning lag-frequency patterns across scales  \n   - Dynamic projection: enforce constraints via function-space projection during interpolation  \n   - This tackles the issue of local trapping by injecting synthetic candidates not derived from parents  \n\nLimitations:  \n   - Requires careful interpolation that maintains step-function properties  \n   - Computationally intensive due to multidimensional interpolation  \n   Demonstrated improvements are expected due to active interference between optimization components and resolution layers  \n\n2. Mathematical specification  \nRepresentation:  \n   Let h: [0,2] \u2192 [0,1] such that \u222bh(x)dx=1  \nScore:  \n   C(h) = sup{ \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx : k\u2208\u211d}  \nDiscretization:  \n   h \u2208 \u211d\u207f, with n power-of-two size  \n   \u2211h_i = n/2 (after projection)  \n   Score \u2248 2/n \u00d7 max_lag( correlation(h, 1-h)(lag) )  \nConstraints:  \n   0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 for all i  \n   \u03a3 h_i = n/2  \n\nOptimization framework:  \n   - Inner loop: adaptive gradient flow with Lagrangian multiplier enforcement (projection)  \n   - Outer loop: hierarchical transfer between manifolds of different resolutions via interpolation preserving the spectral content  \n   - Score evaluation: use finite element approximation with exact projection  \n\n3. Implementation procedure  \nExecution flows:  \n   A. Initialization (within 100s):  \n   1. Get parent solution at n=64 (if available)  \n   2. Set n_max = 512 (target resolution)  \n   3. Generate 10^3 synthetic candidates at n=64 using Ramos-Villatoro transform  \n   4. Select top 10% fittest candidates (lowest score)  \n\n   B. Cascade optimization (sequential resolution increases):  \n   1. Perform spectral analysis on selected candidates using FFT-based metrics (L2 norm of gradients in lag-space)  \n   2. Use hierarchical Lagrangian projection: interpolate solutions while maintaining \u2211h_i = n/2  \n   3. Allocate computation budgets: 60% to parent-driven refinement; 40% to synthetic injection  \n\n   C. Dynamic phase transition:  \n   1. When gradient survival threshold < 0.01: switch interpolation modes from LSQR to random projection  \n   2. When correlation plateaus (3 consecutive iterations): inject GAN-synthesized samples matching prior spectral signature  \n   3. Use Levenberg-Marquardt for local tuning with dynamic damping: \u03b1=0.95 initially, reducing to 0.85 during plateaus  \n\n   D. Final refinement (last 50s):  \n   1. Perform Anderson mixing with step-size halving  \n   2. Test multi-start initializations with shifted-Random-Phase Filter  \n   3. Use quadratic penalization when score between 0.385-0.381  \n\n4. Search choices and variations  \nNominating three hyperparameter profiles conditional on plateau detection:  \n   - Profile A (early): \u03b2=0.95, \u03b3=0.99 (momentum)  \n   - Profile B (mid): \u03b2=0.85, \u03b3=0.96  \n   - Profile C (late): \u03b2=0.75, \u03b3=0.91  \nConditional variation triggers:  \n   \u2022 Score improvement -> active interpolation adjustment  \n   \u2022 New resolution level -> inject synthetic samples matching spectral basis  \n\nMemory management:  \n   Maintain separate memory banks for:  \n   - Best score per resolution (LSQR->Anderson-DC migrates storage)  \n   - Spectral gradient basis (n_max \u00d7 n_max)  \n   - Synthetic sample reservoir (cumulative samples n_max\u00d72)  \n\nFallback mechanisms:  \n   \u2022 Resolution migration preserves score continuity via projection:\u03a6(h) = ||\u2207C(h)|| < tolerance  \n   \u2022 Local repair: if optimization fails, use inverse Thue-Morse filtered projection at target resolution  \n   \u2022 Emergency recovery: revert to most recent function-bound crossover solution  \n\n5. Numerical robustness  \nConstraint handling:  \n   Use \u2265 volume preserving projection: minimize \u2225h - h\u0302\u2225\u2082 subject to \u2211h\u0302_i = n/2  \n   Box constraints enforced via clamping after projection  \nInterpolation:  \n   Adapt interpolation mode based on frequency content:  \n   \u2022 Piecewise cubic Hermite interpolation for smooth regions  \n   \u2022 Random projection for singular values <0.2  \nValidation:  \n   \u2022 Ensure finite score evaluation at every iteration  \n   \u2022 Reject solutions with score rising >1% of best ever computed  \n\n6. Runtime safeguards  \nTermination logic:  \n   \u2192 1100s budget monitor with adaptive iteration cap (n_iter_max = 4\u00d7time_remaining)  \n   \u2192 score improvement halts>5\u00d7original best at each resolution  \n   \u2192 sterile solution detection (7 consecutive worsens) \u2192 abort to nearest valid solution  \nHierarchy handling:  \n   \u2192 Store best solution per resolved dimension for later retrieval  \n   \u2192 Allow dimensionality reduction if base resolution fails to yield convergence  \n   \u2192 guarantee final output has score <best ever = {previous_best()}  \n\n6. Return interface:  \n(h_values, c5_bound, n_points)  \nwhere h_values is step function vector, c5_bound is computed score, n_points = 512 for final output  \nFallback scoring: use simple correlation calculation if complex version fails",
